Title = {Critical review of improved deep reinforcement learning methods for
   online remaining useful life prediction of lithium-ion batteries in
   energy storage stations},
Journal = {RENEWABLE \& SUSTAINABLE ENERGY REVIEWS},
Year = {2027},
Volume = {244},
Number = {A},
Month = {JAN},
Abstract = {Reliable online remaining useful life (RUL) prediction is essential for
   the safe and economical operation of lithium-ion batteries in
   large-scale energy storage systems. The principal contribution of this
   review is an integrated deep reinforcement learning (DRL)-based
   prognostics and health-aware decision framework for organizing advances
   in battery lifespan prediction and management. The framework
   distinguishes direct RUL prediction, prediction-model adaptation, and
   degradation-aware control to avoid treating different outcomes as
   equivalent evidence. It classifies deep Q-network (DQN), proximal policy
   optimization (PPO), other actor-critic algorithms, multi-agent
   reinforcement learning (MARL), and offline reinforcement learning by
   learning mechanism, action space, system scale, and deployment
   conditions. It also links multi-source state construction, temporal
   degradation modeling, physics-informed reward design, uncertainty
   quantification, and pack-level coordination within a unified
   prediction-decision process. Representative studies reported reductions
   of up to 56.12\% in RMSE through adaptive fusion of long short-term
   memory (LSTM) and transformer predictions. Lightweight multi-teacher
   distillation reduced the parameter count by a factor of 36.6 while
   retaining competitive performance on laboratory and electric-vehicle
   datasets. A degradation-aware bidding strategy achieved a computation
   time of 2.47 ms, indicating potential for station-level decision
   support, although the evidence remained numerical and market-specific.
   Overall, improved DRL supports adaptive battery prognostics, but
   standardized evaluation, field validation, model transparency, and
   cross-domain generalization remain essential for practical deployment.},
